and it is not a lecture dedicated to future software programmers either (which I am not by the way). Instead, the aim of this lecture is to provide to the typical chemistry student (or anyone else) the tools to be able to quickly develop a program to solve a given problem.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"aZFbpsWuxd"}],"key":"d5aYQ6CrV3"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Finally, it is worth questioning whether teaching Python courses remains necessary in light of the incredible programming abilities of Artificial Intelligence? As I write these lines (2026), I still think yes, at least to know where to start from: what should I install on my computer to do some programming, what are the possibilities offered by Python, and how does a program works under the hood. AI is an amazing tool for programming, and just anyone can produce very complex programs nowadays in a couple of hours. Therefore, I definitely encourage the use of AI for programming, but I believe that one must know the basics of programming to really get the best out of it. The sub-section ","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"VqbeBXjoD0"},{"type":"link","kind":"ref","identifier":"content:vibecoding","label":"content:VibeCoding","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Some good Vibes... Coding!","key":"w6Kz8PfGw9"}],"url":"/content/vibe-coding","internal":true,"dataUrl":"/content.vibe-coding.json","key":"yUjA9magoo","urlSource":"/content/vibe-coding"},{"type":"text","value":" discusses some aspects of using AI for programming, and will probably occupy a larger and larger part in the years to come.","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"xwgdXPCSNQ"}],"key":"ENo5NBpPhM"},{"type":"admonition","kind":"warning","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Warning","key":"fbDeBJ9fLx"}],"key":"nUfiIGGdDy"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"This textbook is under construction and may change (very) often!","position":{"start":{"line":12,"column":1},"end":{"line":12,"column":1}},"key":"aNFLAqkNLw"}],"key":"Rz3ynZtzR5"}],"key":"G1rfcpUU1K"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Textbook content","position":{"start":{"line":15,"column":1},"end":{"line":15,"column":1}},"key":"dJNYDLYHxI"}],"identifier":"textbook-content","label":"Textbook content","html_id":"textbook-content","implicit":true,"key":"FjEVyGDzZn"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The lecture is essentially based on ","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"Z7m6SHdkkr"},{"type":"strong","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Active Learning","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"wygAZFtwZ6"}],"key":"iZKy94gl5p"},{"type":"text","value":", meaning that you will be actively involved and doing something besides passively listening or watching. In particular, you will have to read many things by yourselves, and you will have the occasion to verify your understanding all along the way with exercices. These exercices always include a solution, and you may also be able to solve them in a couple of seconds using AI. Yet I encourage you to spend sometimes trying to solve them by yourself, as this is (in my opinion) the only way to progress and get a deep understanding of Python.","position":{"start":{"line":17,"column":1},"end":{"line":17,"column":1}},"key":"Uw025WiiVG"}],"key":"SEOkFZD0lw"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":19,"column